 #:seon.task{:name "a-branch-loaded-by-name-reads-without-its-commit-identity"
             :plan "`datahike.versioning/branch-as-db` over a connection answers a value with no committed identity (`datahike.db/committed-value-identity` nil, empty `:cache-context`), while `datahike.versioning/commit-as-db` over the same connection attaches it. So every projection read on a branch loaded by name misses `seon.db/carried-projection`'s commit and revision keys and recomputes `seon.db/content-projection` from its declaration datoms. Measured on default 487388073, warm, on Run 18's `:attempt-0343dd641b00` (F55): `seon.context/step-verdict` 132-242 ms with 21 `seon.db/content-projection` calls (99 ms in `seon.db/declaration-content-key`), against 4.6 ms and none for the same head through `commit-as-db`; a cold miss costs about 7 s each (health: 21 calls, mean 6,852 ms self). Run 18's >20 s and >62 s reads were those cold misses on a JVM one turn proc held at a core. The owner is Datahike's (our fork): load the branch head as `commit-as-db` does, with `attached-cache-context`, in a restart batch; then a branch read by name costs what its commit read costs. Read `datahike.versioning/branch-as-db`, `datahike.versioning/commit-as-db` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/run-18.md`."}
