 #:seon.task{:name "a-reopened-commit-carries-its-content-revisions"
             :plan "Phase H2 of the evidence. Owner-delegated Q2 decision: A, persist the prior revision context and modified attributes in the Datahike commit. The optimized read-through population scan alone costs 31-35 ms and 21.6-24.9 MB for about 714,000 datoms; lineage adds 22-41 ms and 41.9-58.9 MB with a missing ancestor on both measured heads. The live native prior context serializes to 5,439 bytes; its size grows with distinct revised attributes, not datoms or transactions. The bridge already has 58 evictions at the 512 MB budget, so reconstruction is not once per commit per process. A stored Datahike commit carries no revision map, so `datahike.connector/connect` and `datahike.versioning/attached-cache-context` open any commit the connection did not write as the conservative revision and every content-keyed cache misses on a fresh fork; Seon bridges it in memory (`seon.db/remember-revisions!`, kind :seon.db/commit-revisions, the chain walk in `seon.db/content-revision`), lost at eviction and restart. H2a, the Datahike fork: `datahike.writing/commit!` stores with each commit record its revisions, the writer's prior context advanced by the batch's modified attributes at this commit id (`datahike.writing/commit-revisions`, `datahike.db/advance-cache-context`), outside the commit identity's input; `datahike.connector/connect`, `datahike.versioning/attached-cache-context` and `deref-conn` read them (`datahike.writing/stored-revisions`); a record without them names its own commit as the conservative revision, as before, so no migration. The whole map is stored, not a parent delta needing an ancestry walk on load: default's busiest branch holds 161 entries, 5,555 bytes of raw Fressian (2026-10-11). H2b, Seon: `seon.db/remember-revisions!`, `seon.db/recorded-revisions`, the kind and the chain walk are deleted; `seon.db/content-revisions` is a lookup; `seon.cluster.registry/branch!` and `seon.cluster.registry/fork!` stop remembering. Proof: this test: fork from main, write one fork entity, release, reopen twice through fresh connections (`seon.cluster.store/open-branch!`, no process memory of the fork); `seon.db/content-revisions` over `seon.program/program-attributes` equals main's and the written attributes name the fork head (a raw store's value stays detached, Datahike's own test). Files: reference-code/datahike (fork bump), src/seon/db.clj, src/seon/cluster/registry.clj, test/seon/db_test.clj. Read `datahike.writing/create-commit-id`, `datahike.writing/db->stored`, `datahike.writing/stored->db`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/q2-revisions-decision.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-explicit-eid-outside-the-allocators-range-is-refused"
             :plan "Datahike accepts an explicit numeric entity id that can never be an entity. Measured 2026-10-11 through `seon.db/transact!` on a disposable branch of default (retired) with [:db/add eid :db/ident k]: eid tx0+7 (536870919) accepted into the transaction-id range (it collides with a transaction entity); eid 7<<32|1 (30064771073, above emax) accepted, then invisible to `[?e :db/ident ?v]` scans and to the identity lookup by its value, so the next upsert of that value makes a silent duplicate. Same on a raw in-memory db. Fix at the producer, in the fork (reference-code/datahike): `datahike.db.transaction/transact-tx-data` refuses, in the entity position of :db/add, :db/retract and an entity map, a numeric eid above the current transaction id (a transaction not yet issued, or above `datahike.constants/emax`) as an ordinary transaction error (:error :entity-id/range); an issued transaction id stays accepted, since it names that transaction's metadata, which Datahike's own valid-time and tx-meta writes annotate, and `seon.db/transact!` returns it as its declared refusal with no allocator moved. An explicit eid BELOW tx0 that the allocator never issued stays accepted and observed, as `datahike.db.transaction/advance-max-eid` does today: under `seon.db/entities-born-on-two-branches-of-one-commit-have-different-ids` the store's counter advances to it as well, so no later allocation collides; refusing it would break Datahike's own tests, which transact small explicit eids on fresh dbs. Proof: this test (an unissued transaction id and one above emax refuse through `seon.db/transact!` and max-eid is unchanged; an unissued eid below tx0 is accepted and the next fresh entity allocates above it). Files: reference-code/datahike (fork bump), test/seon/db_test.clj. Read `datahike.constants/tx0`, `datahike.db.utils/entid-strict`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/eid-ranges-opus.md`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/eid-ranges-sol.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "entities-born-on-two-branches-of-one-commit-have-different-ids"
             :plan "Phase AW1a of the evidence (owner, 2026-10-11: one global counter per store, incremented as entity ids are handed out, so data is unique from birth and the squash merge replays a new entity's datoms with its eid unchanged). Today `datahike.versioning/branch!` copies the head's max-eid and `datahike.db.transaction/next-eid` is (inc max-eid), so two forks of one commit allocate the same eids (Opus: 60192 vs 60204 later; Sol: both eid 1). The fork change (reference-code/datahike, about 60 lines): (1) `datahike.db.transaction/next-eid` takes the report and allocates one past both max-eid and :datahike.db.transaction/eid-floor on it, the last id the store's AtomicLong reserved when the writer began the transaction; the writer (`datahike.writing/with-store-eids`) then reserves the db-after's max-eid by compareAndSet from that floor and reruns the transaction above the new floor if another branch reserved meanwhile, so a refused or retried transaction spends no ids and a single-branch store numbers exactly as before; its five call sites (`datahike.db.transaction/entity-map->op-vec` twice, `datahike.db.transaction/transact-tx-data` twice, `datahike.db.transaction/transact-entities-directly` once) pass the report they already hold; (2) `datahike.writing/transact!` passes the floor through `datahike.core/with`'s new arity, so the plain `datahike.core/with` (speculation: Seon's d/with test members, `seon.db/with`) and a db with no store keep the local (inc max-eid) and spend no ids; (3) a per-store registry, one AtomicLong keyed by the store id `datahike.gc-guard` already uses, created at the store's first `datahike.connector/connect` in the process and removed by delete-database, seeded from 1 + the max :max-eid over the branches: `k/get` :branches, then per branch `k/get` of its head and `datahike.writing/resolve-head` (a compact head names its commit record), reading the record's :max-eid field, which `datahike.writing/stored-db?` requires of every record Datahike accepts as a head, so no index or datom is loaded: O(branches) small reads once per process; (4) an explicit eid below tx0 above the counter advances the counter as `datahike.db.transaction/advance-max-eid` advances max-eid (observed, never refused), and an eid at or above tx0 refuses (`seon.db/an-explicit-eid-outside-the-allocators-range-is-refused`); a counter reaching tx0 refuses loudly. `max-eid` keeps meaning the branch's observed high-water mark (`datahike.query.estimate/estimate-pattern-with-bindings` now sees a sparse id space: measure plan choices on default's slow queries after). For a single-branch store the counter equals that branch's max-eid at every step, so Datahike's own tests see identical eids; only branch tests can differ. Lurking problems checked: an old record without :max-eid is not a head (stored-db?); a branch whose head is missing raises :head-commit-missing, a corrupt store either way; history, as-of and since values delegate -max-eid to their origin and cannot be written; imports allocate through the same call site; a deleted branch inside the retention window is not in the branches, so an eid that lived only there can be reissued and a by-commit read of that lineage sees the number mean something else there: accepted, an eid is meaningful only within one db value, nothing joins lineages by eid, and the squash needs uniqueness among entities reachable from the branches' heads, which the seed gives; 2^29 ids for the store's life (default holds about 60k), uncommitted allocations are gaps. The mutable cell is the store's eid allocator, increment-only and rederived from committed heads on open, the class of Datahike's own writer state; record it in the ledger (3.19) by that name. Valid because one process root holds the store's lifetime lock. Cost: about 10 ns per eid, no I/O. Prove: in reference-code/datahike `clojure -M:test -m kaocha.runner :clj-pss` (tests.edn; 952 tests when H2 ran it) green; then the fork bump commits as a platform slice with `--test seon.db-test`, `--test seon.cluster.registry-test`, `--test seon.cluster.source-test`. Proofs: this test (two forks of one commit through `seon.cluster.registry/fork!` each transact a new entity through `seon.db/transact!`: the eids differ and are below tx0); `seon.db-test/an-explicit-eid-outside-the-allocators-range-is-refused`; a-reopened-store-allocates-above-every-heads-max-eid (a fresh store closed and reopened allocates above every head's max-eid); cost row: one allocation under 1 us warm through `seon.profile/measure`. Files: reference-code/datahike (fork bump), test/seon/db_test.clj. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/eid-ranges-opus.md`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/eid-ranges-sol.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "inbox-query-evidence-survives-another-recipients-message"
