 #:seon.task{:name "a-contract-property-with-a-qualified-key-is-refused-by-its-digest"
             :plan "A `defn` at an agent's REPL whose contract holds a schema property with a qualified key (`[:=> [:cat [:int {:gen/elements [1 2]}]] :boolean]`, or the shipped `[:any {:seon.schema.admission/exemption ...}]` shape of `seon.schema/malli-form?` and `seon.render.web/server?`) is refused by `seon.db/transact!`: \"The stored definition digest of [:seon.fn/sym ...] is not the one its definition produces\". The same defn with `[:int {:min 1}]` or `[:any {:description \"x\"}]` is admitted. The REPL path's entity producer and `seon.db/definition-digest-of` (through `seon.program/canonical-row`) digest the contract differently. Chain experiment 4 met it twice (attempt 2's two `executor?` defns) and it reproduced on five probe contracts. Correct: the producer digests through the writer's one digest owner, so the stored and produced digests agree. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-4.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-long-defn-after-a-def-of-fn-or-a-namespace-form-change-is-refused-for-its-digest"
             :plan "On agent branches in the Prove and Done-rule REPL tables (2026-10-14), `defn`s that evaluate correctly in SCI are refused by `seon.db/write-digest-error` (\"The stored definition digest of [:seon.fn/sym probe.prove/brief] is not the one its definition produces\", `:seon.error/member` `:seon.program/definition-digest`): `prove-verdict` at its first definition (a `def` of a `(fn ...)` containing `#(some-> ...)`) and the other `def`-of-fn helpers; `brief` and `done-rule` after `(require '[clojure.test :refer ...])` was added to the namespace form and two deftests; and `probe.done.judge/observe` and `done-refusals`, the two long defns whose docstrings contain `->` and `[...]`, on first define (\"exists in your SCI context only\"). Not the retargeted-alias, refer-added, qualified-schema-property or docstring-only causes of `seon.turn.batch-test/a-retargeted-alias-leaves-no-stale-digest-for-the-test-world` (fixed) and the related tasks in this file. Refuted: an auto-required namespace (`clojure.walk`) and an explicit `(require '[clojure.set :refer [union]])` did not refuse once the namespace form listed all its requires up front and every function was redefined in one request, which is the mitigation that held. Shape A reproduced (2026-10-14, agent dd-probe-8): request 1 (ns probe.dd8 (:require [clojure.string :as x] [clojure.test :refer [deftest is]])) (defn f1 [s] (x/upper-case s)) (deftest f1-test (is (= \"A\" (f1 \"a\")))); request 2 (ns-unalias 'probe.dd8 'x) (alias 'x 'clojure.set) (defn g1 [] 2) answers the test world refusal \"program overlay (14 entries) was refused: The stored definition digest of [:seon.fn/sym probe.dd8/f1] ...\": a stale branch digest (cause fixed: a namespace a transaction function writes re-derives its declarations, `seon.turn.batch-test/a-retargeted-alias-leaves-no-stale-digest-for-the-test-world`) that `seon.db/write-digest-error` first sees when `seon.test/program-overlay-tx` copies it into the world. Shape B (the agent's own first-define refusal) did not reproduce under nine shapes listed in `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/digest-and-def-defects.md#Shape B, not reproduced: a first defn refused on the agent's own write`; first form on a new incident: compare each stored digest with `seon.db/definition-digest-of` over the namespace before the refused defn. Cause of shape B not isolated. Correct: find which input the pass's digest and `seon.db/definition-digest-of` read differently for these forms, and make the producer digest through the writer's one owner so the stored and produced digests agree. Read `seon.db/write-digest-error`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/prove-stage-table.md#Surprises` (item 2) and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/done-rule-table.md#Surprise 6: definitions flagged with a digest refusal (inferred, not chased)`. Proof: the deftest a-retargeted-alias-leaves-no-stale-digest-for-the-test-world in seon.turn.batch-test, as drafted in `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/digest-and-def-defects.md`, to commit before assignment."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-refused-deftest-redefinition-is-never-reported-green"
             :plan "A FALSE GREEN (lane merge-repair, 2026-10-14, `seon.ref-test/a-referenced-task-shows-its-intent` redefined on an agent branch). The redefinition was refused \"The stored definition digest ... is not the one its definition produces\" (`seon.db/prepared-write`), yet afterwards `my.program/declaration-diff` still showed the OLD text and `clojure.test/test-vars` answered a reused green result with `:seon.test/unchanged true` for the OLD entity; the new body was only checked by running its assertion directly. An agent is told green for code it did not define. Done when (1) a refused redefinition is answered in the same reply as not installed, naming the old definition as the one still in force, (2) a test result is never answered for a body the branch did not install: the reuse that answers `:seon.test/unchanged` (`seon.test/select`, `seon.test.runner/run-selected-tests`) keys on the digest of the entity the branch holds, and a deftest added in the refused request is shown as missing, never green; proven by a deftest that redefines a test with a body that is refused and asserts the result is not green for the new body. Root cause of the refusal itself: the digest-disagreement tasks already filed in this file (`seon.db/definition-digest-of` and the REPL path's entity producer digest differently); this entry is the false green beside them. Read `seon.db/prepared-write`, `seon.db/definition-digest-of`, `seon.test/select` and `my.program/declaration-diff`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-unknown-pull-attribute-refuses-once-naming-the-attribute"
             :plan "S5 run 1 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/s5-run-1.md#Context defects`): `seon.db/pull` with the attribute `:seon.eval/outcome` (not in the schema) over 10 entities answered each entity with a 46-frame stack chain, about 6,000 tokens; one refusal naming the attribute would do. Done when `seon.db/pull` and `seon.db/pull-many` with an attribute the schema does not declare return one refusal per call, `:seon.error/type` naming the attribute and its nearest declared names (`seon.transact-feedback-test/uninstalled-attribute-candidates-are-the-nearest-names` is the sibling behavior for writes), carrying no stack frames, proven by a row in an existing `seon.db-test` case that pulls an undeclared attribute over several entities and asserts one refusal. Read `seon.db/pull` and `seon.db/pull-many`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "evidence-read-on-one-branch-is-not-current-on-another"
             :plan "`seon.db/read-evidence-current?` answers true for a read made on main when it is checked on another branch's commit whose datoms for that read differ. Reproduced on default 2026-10-16 (lane cache-plan-opus, JVM root, read-only): `[:find ?e ?s :where [?e :seon.fn/form-span ?s]]` read on main, checked on `(seon.db/branch-database main :chain-68d99c8aef55)`: 34 main-only and 34 branch-only results, and the check still answered true. Cause: `seon.db/branch-database` and `seon.db/commit-database` load the commit with `datahike.api/commit-as-db`, which gives the value the caller's connection id and generation (`datahike.versioning/attached-cache-context`). `seon.db/read-evidence-current?` then treats the two branches as one lineage, and its fallback runs `d/since` from the read's basis-t. Transaction numbers overlap across branches: the branch-only datoms have tx 536872590, below main's basis 536874187. The since view misses them. The proof that holds across branches is `datahike.db/datoms-diff` between the read's basis commit and the reader over the read's attributes, which skips shared subtrees (0.05 ms per attribute on a near branch). Measured in `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-16/derivation-cache/opus.md`. Done when a new `seon.read-evidence-test` row reads on one `d/branch!` and checks on a sibling that differs below the basis t, answering false, and when `seon.read-evidence-test/a-fresh-connection-keeps-an-unrelated-read-current` stays green."}
